--TEST--
Core\Csv::parse's {escape:} byte is consumed and the byte after it is taken literally, where str_getcsv leaves both in the field and only declines to end the record on them
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
PHP's `$escape` is not an escape: `str_getcsv` uses the byte only to decide that the *next* byte does not close the quoted field, and then hands back both bytes unchanged, so `"a\"b"` parses to `a\"b` and there is no spelling in PHP for the field `a"b` written that way — Novis consumes the escape byte and answers `a"b`, which is the reading `{escape:}` is asked for in the first place and the only one that makes `\` a dialect knob rather than a record-terminator hint.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/csv.rs`'s module doc records that RFC 4180's dialect —
// no escape byte at all, an inner quote written by doubling it — is the default
// on both sides, and `csv-parse-reads-one-record-exactly-as-str_getcsv-does`
// pins the whole field grammar under it. This case is the one knob where
// turning it makes the two languages answer different fields rather than the
// same field a different way.
final class Show {
    public static function of(array<string> $row): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($row as string $field) {
            $out = $out . "[" . $field . "]";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// `"a\"b",c`. PHP answers `a\"b` — the backslash and the quote both survive,
// and the quote did not end the field, which is the entire effect the byte has
// there. Novis answers `a"b`.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("\"a\\\"b\",c", {escape: "\\"})["0"]), "\n";

// `"a\\b",c`. PHP answers `a\\b`, keeping both bytes again; Novis takes the
// second backslash as the escaped one and answers `a\b`. So the escape byte is
// never removable in PHP, which is why a document round-tripped through
// `fputcsv` and `str_getcsv` grows one backslash per escape each time.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("\"a\\\\b\",c", {escape: "\\"})["0"]), "\n";

// Outside a quoted field the byte is ordinary on both sides, because there is
// no quote for it to be deciding anything about. This row is the agreement that
// bounds the divergence above.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("a\\b,c", {escape: "\\"})["0"]), "\n";

// Doubling keeps working alongside the escape byte rather than being replaced
// by it, so a document written by the RFC 4180 half still reads under the
// legacy dialect — and here the two languages agree, since neither side has an
// escape byte to keep.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("\"a\"\"b\",c", {escape: "\\"})["0"]), "\n";

// And with the default dialect the escape byte has no meaning at all: `\` is a
// field byte like any other and the doubled quote is still the inner one.
echo Show::of(Core\Csv::parse("\"a\\\"\"b\",c")["0"]), "\n";
--EXPECT--
[a"b][c]
[a\b][c]
[a\b][c]
[a"b][c]
[a\"b][c]
